  m_Name: FireTubeBoilerData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Fire Tube Boiler
  engineDescription: A Fire Tube Boiler is a steam-generating device in which hot
    combustion gases flow through tubes surrounded by water, transferring heat to
    the water and producing steam. Its main components include the furnace, boiler
    shell, fire tubes, burner or grate, steam space, water space, chimney, safety
    valve, feedwater system, and pressure controls. During operation, fuel is burned
    inside the furnace to produce high-temperature combustion gases. These gases
    pass through the fire tubes while water surrounds the tubes inside the boiler
    shell. Heat is transferred from the hot gases through the tube walls to the surrounding
    water, causing the water to boil and generate steam. The steam collects in the
    upper portion of the boiler and is supplied to the required process or machinery.
    Fire tube boilers are widely used in small and medium-sized industrial plants,
    heating systems, process industries, and low-to-moderate-pressure steam applications.
    From an engineering perspective, understanding combustion, heat transfer, steam
    generation, pressure control, water treatment, thermal efficiency, circulation,
    tube fouling, corrosion, and safety systems is essential for safe and efficient
    boiler operation.
  engineCategory: Engineering
